# How Do I Use Metarhizium and Mycopesticides for Pest Control?

## Short Answer

Mycopesticides are biological pest control products based on entomopathogenic fungi that naturally infect and kill insect pests.
The most widely used genera include [[metarhizium]] and [[beauveria]], which attack pests by penetrating their exoskeleton and consuming them from within.
These fungal agents offer an organic alternative to chemical pesticides, targeting specific pest species while sparing beneficial insects like pollinators and predators.

## What Are Mycopesticides?

Mycopesticides are formulations of living fungal spores or mycelial preparations applied to crops to manage insect populations.
The term encompasses commercial products and homemade preparations derived from [[entomopathogenic-fungi]].
Paul Stamets popularized the concept through his work on [[mycopesticides-stamets]] and [[presporulating-mycelium-trojan-horse-mycopesticides]].
These fungi have evolved over millions of years to detect, attach to, and invade insect hosts with remarkable precision.
The global mycopesticide market has grown significantly as organic farming regulations restrict synthetic chemical options.

## How Does Metarhizium Work?

[[metarhizium-anisopliae]] is the most commercially important species in this genus, effective against over 200 insect species.
The infection process begins when fungal conidia (spores) attach to the insect cuticle upon contact.
Once attached, the spore germinates and produces specialized structures called appressoria that physically breach the exoskeleton.
Inside the insect body, the fungus produces blastospores that colonize the hemolymph and consume nutrients.
Death typically occurs within 3 to 7 days, after which the fungus emerges to release new spores.
[[metarhizium-flavoviride]] is another species used primarily against grasshoppers and locusts in arid regions.

## Key Mycopesticide Fungal Species

Several fungal species form the backbone of mycopesticide development and commercial production.
[[beauveria-bassiana]] targets whiteflies, aphids, thrips, and beetle larvae in both field and greenhouse settings.
[[beauveria-brongniartii]] specializes against soil-dwelling pests like white grubs and wireworms.
[[cordyceps-and-entomopathogenic-fungi]] represents a related group that includes species with both medicinal and insecticidal properties.
[[entomopathogenic-fungal-diversity-cordyceps-insect-pathogens]] shows the remarkable breadth of fungi that have evolved to attack insects.
The [[comparison-beauveria-bassiana-vs-metarhizium-anisopliae|comparison between Beauveria and Metarhizium]] highlights that Beauveria works better in humid conditions while Metarhizium tolerates drier environments.

## Application Methods

Mycopesticides can be applied as foliar sprays, soil drenches, or seed treatments depending on the target pest.
Foliar applications work best when humidity is high and temperatures range between 20 and 30 degrees Celsius.
Soil drenches target root-feeding pests and benefit from the natural soil moisture that supports fungal survival.
[[aact-trichoderma-biocontrol-ingham|Compost tea approaches]] can be adapted to cultivate and deliver entomopathogenic fungi.
The [[ingham-aact-beauveria-fruit-fly-beneficial-bacteria-inoculant|Ingham Beauveria inoculant method]] demonstrates how to culture these fungi in aerated compost tea.
[[ingham-beauveria-parasitic-fungi|Ingham's parasitic fungi research]] provides protocols for multiplying fungal biocontrol agents on-farm.
Always apply mycopesticides in the early morning or late evening to protect spores from UV degradation.

## Advantages Over Chemical Pesticides

Mycopesticides decompose naturally and leave no toxic residues on crops or in the environment.
They can be integrated into organic certification programs that prohibit synthetic chemical inputs.
Pest resistance develops more slowly because the fungi use multiple infection mechanisms simultaneously.
[[entomopathogenic-fungi-and-biological-pest-control]] research shows that fungal agents can establish in ecosystems and provide ongoing suppression.
Many beneficial insects are less susceptible to mycopesticides than to broad-spectrum chemical sprays.
[[mycopesticides-fungal-biocontrol]] formulations can be combined with other organic methods for integrated pest management.

## Limitations and Challenges

Mycopesticides require specific environmental conditions to be effective, particularly adequate moisture and moderate temperatures.
They act more slowly than chemical pesticides, typically taking several days to kill target pests.
Shelf life is limited because living fungal spores degrade over time, especially in hot or humid storage.
[[mycopesticide-development]] faces regulatory hurdles that vary by country and can slow product commercialization.
Commercial products can be expensive compared to conventional pesticides, though homemade preparations reduce costs.
UV radiation rapidly kills exposed spores, necessitating careful timing of applications or formulation with UV protectants.

## Integration with Other Biocontrol Methods

Mycopesticides work best as part of a comprehensive biological control strategy rather than a standalone treatment.
[[comparison-trichoderma-vs-entomopathogenic-fungi|Trichoderma and entomopathogenic fungi]] can be used together to target both plant pathogens and insect pests.
[[entomopathogenic-nematodes-biological-pest-control]] provides a complementary approach for soil-dwelling pests.
[[query-how-do-i-use-biological-control-to-manage-pests-organically|Biological control methods]] should be combined with cultural practices like crop rotation and habitat management.
[[beneficial-fungi-trichoderma-soil-health|Beneficial Trichoderma]] can coexist with entomopathogenic fungi in the soil ecosystem.
Companion planting with insectary plants supports natural enemies that work alongside mycopesticide applications.

## What About Making My Own Mycopesticides?

Growers can cultivate entomopathogenic fungi from infected insect specimens found in the field.
[[mycelium-running-presporulating-mycopesticides|Stamets' presporulating mycelium method]] allows cultivation on grain substrates for field application.
The process involves collecting diseased insects, isolating the fungus on petri plates, and scaling up on sterile grain.
[[unknown-mycelium-mycopesticides]] research suggests many wild fungal isolates have untapped pest control potential.
Homemade preparations must be used fresh because spore viability declines rapidly without proper drying and storage.
Always test homemade preparations on a small area before wide-scale application to confirm efficacy.

## What About Targeting Specific Pests?

Different mycopesticide species and strains have evolved to specialize against particular insect groups.
Metarhizium acridum (formerly a variety of M. anisopliae) specifically targets grasshoppers and locusts with devastating efficiency.
Beauveria bassiana strains vary widely in virulence against different hosts, so matching strain to pest is essential.
For cannabis growers, [[cervantes-trichoderma-beneficial-fungi|Trichoderma species]] complement mycopesticides by protecting roots while above-ground applications target foliage pests.
Thrips, fungus gnats, and spider mites are among the most common greenhouse pests controlled by mycopesticide applications.
Mosquito larvae in standing water can be targeted with Metarhizium formulations applied as floating briquettes or granules.

## Storage and Handling Best Practices

Commercial mycopesticide products should be stored in cool, dry conditions away from direct sunlight.
Most spore-based formulations remain viable for 6 to 18 months when stored at 4 degrees Celsius.
Freezing can damage some formulations, so check manufacturer guidelines before refrigerating or freezing products.
Always use clean equipment when mixing and applying mycopesticides to avoid contamination that reduces efficacy.
Tank-mixing with fungicides is generally not recommended because fungicides will kill the beneficial fungal spores.

